\section{The non-identifiability obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Non-identifiability of the coverage parameter]\label{thm:nonid}
For every $\Lambda,\Theta,S>0$ there exists $q>0$ with
$B(\Lambda,\Theta,q) = S$, namely $q = 1/(\Lambda\Theta S)$; and this $q$ is
unique among positive reals.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence by substitution; uniqueness because $q\mapsto1/(\Lambda\Theta q)$ is
injective on $(0,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Anchor inversion is a tautology]
For every $S>0$, $B(1,1,1/S) = S$. Reading off parameters at
$\Lambda=\Theta=1$ through $S = 1/\hat q$ reproduces the observed speed-up
exactly, whatever the observation.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:nonid} says the master inequality with $\hat q$ free is
\emph{unfalsifiable}: no observed speed-up can contradict it, because the
fitting procedure has one free parameter that absorbs any residual. An anchor
calibrated this way is not a test of tightness but an algebraic restatement of
the datum. Concretely, all four legacy anchors had their parameters obtained by
inverting the law through $S_A = 1/\hat q$ at $\Lambda=\Theta=1$; the residual
is machine-zero and one anchor reproduces its booked value to $5\times10^{-7}$
--- exactly what an exact self-consistent inversion does, and exactly what
conveys no information. Anchor tightness is therefore \emph{not currently
decidable}; it becomes decidable only with a raw, non-inverted effective
profile measured under a pre-committed protocol. The general moral: a parameter
fitted by inverting a law cannot be used to test that law.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Slack from a profile.}
Compute $\casc = \sum_i(i+1)p_i$ in $O(M)$; then
$\cdesc = M+1-\casc$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:cons} in $O(1)$, $C_0 = (M+1)/2$, and
$\Lambda,\Theta,X,\Sasc,B$ in $O(1)$. Total $O(M)$ time and $O(1)$ extra
space; the conservation identity halves the arithmetic and removes any
possibility of the two costs being inconsistently computed.

\paragraph{Exhaustive policy audit.}
Enumerate all $M!$ policies, compute each cost, and confirm that the minimum
equals $\casc$, that it is attained uniquely, and that
$\max_\sigma S(p,\sigma)\cdot X = B$ to machine precision. Cost
$O(M!\cdot M)$, feasible for $M\le9$; a direct check of
Theorems~\ref{thm:rear}, \ref{thm:uniq} and \ref{thm:unattain}.

\paragraph{Interval transfer.}
Since $\lambda\mapsto(1+\lambda)/(2\lambda)$ is strictly decreasing, a
confidence interval $[\lambda_-,\lambda_+]$ maps to
$X\in[g(\lambda_+),g(\lambda_-)]$ by endpoint evaluation in $O(1)$: exact, not
asymptotic, and requiring no sampling.

\paragraph{Constrained extremal search.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:polytope} replaces an $O(\mathrm{poly}(M))$ linear-programming
solve by the closed form $\frac{M+1}{2Km+2}$, attained by an explicit two-cell
profile.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Ordered search and scheduling.}
The framework applies to any sequential search whose cost is the rank at which
the target is found: linear probing with a skewed key distribution, cache line
scanning, disk-block search, priority scheduling with a known arrival profile.
The conservation identity and the fibration hold in all of them, so ``slack
against the ideal bound'' is always a one-dimensional quantity determined by the
first moment.

\paragraph{Benchmark interpretation.}
The results explain why a well-tuned algorithm can persistently fall short of a
proven upper bound without being suboptimal. If the tightness case of a bound is
degenerate, the measured shortfall is a \emph{measurement of non-degeneracy},
not a performance deficit; reporting $X$ alongside $S$ separates the two.

\paragraph{Statistics of inverted anchors.}
Whenever a nuisance parameter is calibrated by inverting the relation under
test, the resulting ``confirmation'' has zero evidential content.
Pre-registration or independent measurement of the nuisance parameter is the
only remedy.

\paragraph{One-sided grid reading.}
A coarse estimate errs in a known direction, so reported slacks may be used as
lower bounds without a discretisation error budget.

\paragraph{Limits of scope.}
The analysis concerns the \emph{shape} layer --- the density of the positional
prior --- and is orthogonal to rate-layer questions such as throughput
plateaus. It uses only the density, and no sequence structure or correlations
between successive targets.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{From the dyadic refinement to the continuum limit.} The grid
dependence of the slack sits in a single scalar identity, so the refinement
comparison reduces to the sign of $\sum_j(g_{2j}-g_{2j+1})$: cell merging moves
the measured mean position forward by exactly
$\frac1{4M}\sum_j(g_{2j}-g_{2j+1})$, non-negative precisely for a front-loaded
profile. The dyadic step and the scalar monotonicity are theorems. Still open
is the same statement for cell-averaging a fixed \emph{continuum} density onto
grids of arbitrary (non-dyadic) size, with the limit $X_M\to1/(2E)$ along that
family.
\item \textbf{Multiple linear constraints.} Theorem~\ref{thm:polytope} handles
a single tail constraint and produces a two-cell extremal profile. With several
linear constraints (multiple tail masses, moment bounds, a monotonicity
envelope), the extremal profile should be supported on at most one more cell
than the number of constraints; the exact reachable interval and extremal
supports are open.
\item \textbf{Correlated and adaptive settings.} The present cost functional
assumes a single target from a fixed prior and a non-adaptive policy. Whether
an analogue of the conservation identity survives adaptivity or correlation
between successive targets is open.
\item \textbf{The decidable closer.} A joint measurement of speed-up, shape and
alignment on the recorded positional data under the window-ascending policy is
predicted to give $S\approx1.31$ against a bound of $1.51$; observing $S$
appreciably above $1.51$ would falsify the mapping. This is a genuine two-sided
test requiring no new instrumentation.
\item \textbf{Non-inverted anchor measurement.} Deciding anchor tightness
requires a raw effective profile measured under a pre-committed protocol, with
the coverage parameter measured independently or fixed in advance. Until then
Theorem~\ref{thm:nonid} forbids any inference from anchor agreement.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A single conservation identity --- forward plus backward scan cost equals twice
the flat baseline --- collapses the two-parameter tightness question into one
scalar, the slack factor $X = C_0/\casc = 1/\Theta = (1+\Lambda)/(2\Lambda)$,
and shows that the bound factors exactly as $B = X\cdot\Sasc$. Front-loading
plus non-flatness forces $X>1$ strictly, so no realizable policy attains the
bound; yet the two-cell family drives $X$ to $1$, so the bound is sharp over
the prior class. The overshoot is quantified by an optimal dispersion
inequality, is determined solely by the mean probe position, ranges over an
exactly characterised interval (also under a tail-mass constraint), is
under-estimated by any finite grid, and is forced for every window ratio by the
harmonic shape of the measured profile. Finally, the coverage parameter's
non-identifiability voids the historical anchor evidence for tightness.

On the measured data $X\approx1.153\in[1.102,1.221]$: the proven bound
overshoots every realizable policy by between $10\%$ and $22\%$, and the
overshoot is profile-forced and policy-independent. The inequality is not
weakened by this. It is located.

